WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:04.070
-Our next guests are three very
talented comedians whose show,

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:05.905
"Los Espookys,"
is back for a second season.

00:00:05.905 --> 00:00:08.207
You can see new episodes Fridays
at 11:00 p.m.

00:00:08.207 --> 00:00:09.909
on HBO and HBO Max.

00:00:09.909 --> 00:00:11.010
Let's take a look.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:14.014
♪♪

00:00:41.508 --> 00:00:45.011
Please welcome back to the show
our very own Fred Armisen,

00:00:45.011 --> 00:00:48.081
along with Ana Fabrega
and Julio Torres.

00:00:48.081 --> 00:00:51.051
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:01:03.296 --> 00:01:04.497
Welcome back.

00:01:04.497 --> 00:01:06.633
I'm so happy to have
all three of you here.

00:01:06.633 --> 00:01:10.269
This is one of
the more unique shows on TV.

00:01:10.269 --> 00:01:14.707
I would like to ask each of you
how you would describe

00:01:14.707 --> 00:01:18.344
"Los Espookys" if someone
hadn't seen it yet.

00:01:18.344 --> 00:01:21.247
-If it starts with me --
let's see -- I would say --

00:01:21.247 --> 00:01:24.517
-It does.
-Yeah.

00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:26.386
-A group of friends,
four friends,

00:01:26.386 --> 00:01:28.755
have a business
where they get hired

00:01:28.755 --> 00:01:31.324
to fool people and scare people.

00:01:31.324 --> 00:01:32.492
-Okay.

00:01:32.492 --> 00:01:36.295
-I would say it's about
a group of friends

00:01:36.295 --> 00:01:42.335
who have a business
where they help trick people.

00:01:42.335 --> 00:01:44.938
-Pretty similar so far.
-Uh-huh.

00:01:44.938 --> 00:01:51.610
-It's a half-hour comedy...

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:54.547
on HBO and/or HBO Max.

00:01:54.547 --> 00:01:55.715
-This is good.

00:01:55.715 --> 00:01:57.550
See, this is helpful,
'cause his, like --

00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:59.452
He's got details for me.

00:01:59.452 --> 00:02:04.723
-Uh, first season, 2019.

00:02:04.723 --> 00:02:06.125
How much per episode?

00:02:06.125 --> 00:02:08.894
How much of it --
I was thinking about the budget.

00:02:08.894 --> 00:02:12.231
-Oh.

00:02:12.231 --> 00:02:17.036
-And it is -- It's a --
It's sort of like a --

00:02:17.036 --> 00:02:18.971
like a cartoon for adults.

00:02:18.971 --> 00:02:20.172
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:02:20.172 --> 00:02:22.575
-I think it has the absurdity
of a cartoon.

00:02:22.575 --> 00:02:25.478
You mentioned Season 1 was 2019,
and this is --

00:02:25.478 --> 00:02:28.380
I don't think most shows
have a gap like this.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:30.049
I find it incredibly comforting

00:02:30.049 --> 00:02:32.552
to come back
into the second season.

00:02:32.552 --> 00:02:34.320
Is it different writing
a second season?

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:36.021
Because, again, it's so --

00:02:36.021 --> 00:02:39.125
You did so much world building
in the first season.

00:02:39.125 --> 00:02:42.094
Was it nice to just sort of
write the characters now?

00:02:42.094 --> 00:02:44.797
-Yeah. I mean, and, also,
a big part of the break

00:02:44.797 --> 00:02:46.632
was just we couldn't find
the scripts.

00:02:46.632 --> 00:02:48.468
Like, after we wrote
the second season --

00:02:48.468 --> 00:02:49.736
-Yeah, we lost the scripts.

00:02:49.736 --> 00:02:51.136
-Yeah, it was like,
"I thought you had the scripts."

00:02:51.136 --> 00:02:53.506
"I thought you had the scripts."
-Like, "I don't have them."

00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:54.841
-And then, three years later,

00:02:54.841 --> 00:02:56.375
"Oh, okay,
we found the scripts."

00:02:56.375 --> 00:02:57.676
-Gotcha. So there was
just the one

00:02:57.676 --> 00:02:59.045
hard copy of the scripts.

00:02:59.045 --> 00:03:00.446
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:03:00.446 --> 00:03:03.116
-It wasn't like a thing you
had e-mailed to each other.

00:03:03.116 --> 00:03:05.284
-No, because we're, like --

00:03:05.284 --> 00:03:07.153
You know, HBO and
confidentiality, it's so scary.

00:03:07.153 --> 00:03:10.022
-Oh, yeah, a huge deal. Right.
-So it's like --

00:03:10.022 --> 00:03:12.592
-It's like Marvel movies,
the "Los Espookys" scripts.

00:03:12.592 --> 00:03:15.027
-Yeah. Yeah. Yeah, yeah, yeah.
Oh, yeah.

00:03:15.027 --> 00:03:18.398
If anyone got a hand -- What?
-It kind of is Marvel movies.

00:03:18.398 --> 00:03:20.432
Really, right?
-Yeah, yeah, in a way.

00:03:20.432 --> 00:03:22.901
-So this happens in the
Marvel Universe, "Los Espookys"?

00:03:22.901 --> 00:03:24.403
-I think so.
-Oh, sure. Yeah.

00:03:24.403 --> 00:03:28.040
-In, like, a sub-segment of it.
-Yeah.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:29.576
-Do the people who control
the Marvel properties

00:03:29.576 --> 00:03:31.143
have any idea
that you've put your show

00:03:31.143 --> 00:03:32.611
in the Marvel Universe?

00:03:32.611 --> 00:03:34.113
-Well, they don't decide
what goes into

00:03:34.113 --> 00:03:35.214
the Marvel universe.

00:03:35.214 --> 00:03:36.615
-That's true. Right.
-Yeah.

00:03:36.615 --> 00:03:39.117
-They can build it, but they
don't decide who comes and goes.

00:03:39.117 --> 00:03:41.019
-I'm truly waiting for,
like, the Pillsbury Doughboy

00:03:41.019 --> 00:03:42.388
to join the Avengers.

00:03:42.388 --> 00:03:43.723
-Yeah.

00:03:43.723 --> 00:03:46.759
-You actually did have
an issue with Betty Boop,

00:03:46.759 --> 00:03:48.995
trying to put Betty Boop
into the show.

00:03:48.995 --> 00:03:50.262
Not as easy as you thought.

00:03:50.262 --> 00:03:54.667
Not as cheap
as you thought it might be.

00:03:54.667 --> 00:03:57.103
-No.
-So -- You go.

00:03:57.103 --> 00:03:58.771
-We're big fans of Betty Boop.

00:03:58.771 --> 00:04:00.940
-Of course.
It's your generation.

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:03.276
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:04:03.276 --> 00:04:06.245
-And we knew we wanted to get
Betty involved in Season 2.

00:04:06.245 --> 00:04:07.380
-Right.

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:10.449
-And so we wrote
this little thing thinking

00:04:10.449 --> 00:04:13.986
we'll be able to have it
animated and show Betty Boop.

00:04:13.986 --> 00:04:15.922
Turns out,
way out of our budget.

00:04:15.922 --> 00:04:17.323
-Really?
-Yeah.

00:04:17.323 --> 00:04:20.359
-Even today, when it feels like
no one is trying to use

00:04:20.359 --> 00:04:21.760
Betty Boop.

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:22.829
-Oh, no.

00:04:22.829 --> 00:04:25.497
And see, like,
we like Betty Boop --

00:04:25.497 --> 00:04:26.765
or at least I know that I do --

00:04:26.765 --> 00:04:30.403
because she's inexplicably
a Latin icon.

00:04:30.403 --> 00:04:34.073
-Yeah. If you go South,

00:04:34.073 --> 00:04:35.975
like, truly,
you'll see her everywhere.

00:04:35.975 --> 00:04:36.976
-Oh, interesting.
-Yeah.

00:04:36.976 --> 00:04:38.377
And we're talking --
Just to clarify,

00:04:38.377 --> 00:04:43.749
we're talking about the 1930s or
'40s black-and-white, like,

00:04:43.749 --> 00:04:48.020
sexy "ooh-ooh," like, cartoon.

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:51.523
-And, so, in modern
South America, she's a big deal.

00:04:51.523 --> 00:04:52.791
-Oh, yeah.

00:04:52.791 --> 00:04:55.060
You see a Betty Boop
sticker on a car,

00:04:55.060 --> 00:05:00.165
you know the racial profile of
the person who --

00:05:00.165 --> 00:05:03.135
-So, you reached out,
and the Boop estate was like,

00:05:03.135 --> 00:05:04.570
"You can't even begin."

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:06.238
-The Boop estate was like,
"It's a --"

00:05:06.238 --> 00:05:07.740
-Nonstarter.
-Yeah.

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:09.609
-For how much money? No.
-Yeah.

00:05:09.609 --> 00:05:12.411
-And then, you know,
HBO could have been like,

00:05:12.411 --> 00:05:13.746
"Well, do you want
Nicole Kidman?

00:05:13.746 --> 00:05:16.148
We can afford that."
You know?

00:05:16.148 --> 00:05:18.684
Like, "We need Betty."

00:05:18.684 --> 00:05:20.786
-So, the show, again,
because it's very surreal,

00:05:20.786 --> 00:05:23.289
it's very absurd, sometimes,
you, as writers,

00:05:23.289 --> 00:05:26.392
will write something
that maybe you

00:05:26.392 --> 00:05:27.559
don't quite appreciate how

00:05:27.559 --> 00:05:30.930
technically difficult
it will be.

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:33.466
Sometimes, like,
was there an issue with

00:05:33.466 --> 00:05:35.000
a butterfly and thinking it
would be easier

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:36.335
to use a butterfly
than it seemed?

00:05:36.335 --> 00:05:39.905
-Yeah. There's a moment
where we wanted to have

00:05:39.905 --> 00:05:41.507
a live butterfly
land on my nose.

00:05:41.507 --> 00:05:42.642
-Uh-huh.

00:05:42.642 --> 00:05:44.510
-And we talked
to the animal person,

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:47.079
who was like,
"You can't train a butterfly."

00:05:47.079 --> 00:05:50.516
And we're like,
"But surely there's a way

00:05:50.516 --> 00:05:53.486
someone can teach a butterfly
to land on my nose.

00:05:53.486 --> 00:05:54.820
Or what if
we shoot it backwards?

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:55.922
We put honey on my nose.

00:05:55.922 --> 00:05:57.557
We start with
the butterfly on my nose.

00:05:57.557 --> 00:05:59.258
The butterfly flies off.
We reverse it."

00:05:59.258 --> 00:06:01.894
And they were like,
"You can't train a butterfly."

00:06:01.894 --> 00:06:04.596
-By the way,
problem solver right there?

00:06:04.596 --> 00:06:06.198
Like, would have
never thought of that.

00:06:06.198 --> 00:06:09.768
But, like,
because my pet peeve --

00:06:09.768 --> 00:06:10.802
And I think
we talked about this.

00:06:10.802 --> 00:06:12.138
My pet peeve --

00:06:12.138 --> 00:06:15.174
One of my biggest pet peeves
is CGI butterflies.

00:06:15.174 --> 00:06:19.111
-Yeah. You can always tell.
-And, like, they're everywhere.

00:06:19.111 --> 00:06:20.312
-And they look bad.

00:06:20.312 --> 00:06:22.682
-They are everywhere.
-It's an infestation.

00:06:22.682 --> 00:06:24.750
It's a digital infestation.
-It is.

00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:27.953
It's like,
"Oh, it's now springtime,

00:06:27.953 --> 00:06:29.855
and here comes
the CGI butterfly."

00:06:29.855 --> 00:06:33.459
-Yeah. And we didn't want to be
one of those shows doing that.

00:06:33.459 --> 00:06:35.395
But then we had no choice.

00:06:35.395 --> 00:06:38.297
-Was the honey
to lure the butterfly

00:06:38.297 --> 00:06:39.699
or to stick the butterfly?

00:06:39.699 --> 00:06:41.366
-Both.
-Okay. It's both.

00:06:41.366 --> 00:06:43.836
-But then was the idea that it
would have to eat away

00:06:43.836 --> 00:06:45.772
at the part that was
sticking its legs?

00:06:45.772 --> 00:06:47.873
-I did not think this through.
-Okay.

00:06:47.873 --> 00:06:50.476
-It was, like, in theory,
you would think

00:06:50.476 --> 00:06:51.744
a butterfly eats honey, maybe?

00:06:51.744 --> 00:06:53.846
-Yeah. Right.

00:06:53.846 --> 00:06:55.014
-Are all bugs?

00:06:55.014 --> 00:06:56.115
I think all bugs are supposed to
eat honey, right?

00:06:56.115 --> 00:06:57.884
-Yeah, they all, like --
-Based on --

00:06:57.884 --> 00:06:59.251
-But the bees manufacture it.
-I know. That's what I'm saying.

00:06:59.251 --> 00:07:00.987
Like, why do we all think that?
Because of bees.

00:07:00.987 --> 00:07:03.289
-The bees don't eat it.
-The bees don't eat it?

00:07:03.289 --> 00:07:04.957
-They make it
for the other insects.

00:07:04.957 --> 00:07:06.825
-Yeah.

00:07:06.825 --> 00:07:11.964
-Oh. So they're like a vegan
that owns a butcher shop.

00:07:11.964 --> 00:07:13.499
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:07:13.499 --> 00:07:15.601
-It's like, "I won't touch it,
but I'll sell it."

00:07:15.601 --> 00:07:17.703
-Yeah.
-Okay.

00:07:17.703 --> 00:07:20.239
-Yeah.

00:07:20.239 --> 00:07:24.276
-You -- There --
You mentioned an animal wrangler

00:07:24.276 --> 00:07:27.313
being in charge of the
butterflies and all the animals.

00:07:27.313 --> 00:07:28.447
-Too many animals on our show.

00:07:28.447 --> 00:07:30.616
-We had a surprising
amount of animals.

00:07:30.616 --> 00:07:33.752
-Gotcha. And how do you feel
in general about using them?

00:07:33.752 --> 00:07:37.556
-You know, I --
There's a horse this season.

00:07:37.556 --> 00:07:38.624
-A horse, yeah.

00:07:38.624 --> 00:07:42.327
-That I rode for,
I'm going to say,

00:07:42.327 --> 00:07:44.230
two seconds before
the stunt double takes over.

00:07:44.230 --> 00:07:45.564
-Yeah.

00:07:45.564 --> 00:07:48.201
-It was truly just like me
getting on the horse, cut.

00:07:48.201 --> 00:07:49.434
-Yeah.

00:07:49.434 --> 00:07:52.738
-Fully, like, a woman in a wig
riding a horse.

00:07:52.738 --> 00:07:55.307
-Yeah.
-But I don't know.

00:07:55.307 --> 00:07:59.812
I felt kind of, like, weird that
no one asked the horse

00:07:59.812 --> 00:08:00.847
if the horse wanted to be
on the show.

00:08:00.847 --> 00:08:01.981
-Yeah.

00:08:01.981 --> 00:08:03.215
-Yeah, because you could tell --

00:08:03.215 --> 00:08:05.685
You know, the horse comes with
their wrangler.

00:08:05.685 --> 00:08:08.153
And the wrangler
wanted the horse to be there,

00:08:08.153 --> 00:08:09.355
but the horse was like --

00:08:09.355 --> 00:08:11.657
-The wrangler was like,
"The horse loves it.

00:08:11.657 --> 00:08:13.526
The horse loves it."

00:08:13.526 --> 00:08:15.060
-We don't know if the horse --
-How can you tell?

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:17.329
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:08:17.329 --> 00:08:18.830
-That it's happy.

00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:21.066
-I guess if it comes back for
Season 3, you know it liked it.

00:08:21.066 --> 00:08:24.102
-Do you ever -- Are there any
horses on your crew?

00:08:24.102 --> 00:08:25.738
-We don't have any horses
on the crew, no.

00:08:25.738 --> 00:08:27.706
We did,
but then there was an issue.

00:08:27.706 --> 00:08:28.741
-COVID.
-Yeah, COVID.

00:08:28.741 --> 00:08:30.510
-COVID. Yeah.

00:08:30.510 --> 00:08:33.412
-With a horse,
the masks are impossible,

00:08:33.412 --> 00:08:35.480
because just the elastic
has to go so --

00:08:35.480 --> 00:08:38.317
-Yeah.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:08:38.317 --> 00:08:41.787
-Well, whatever -- I will say,
the result, it paid off.

00:08:41.787 --> 00:08:44.122
You obviously all know what
you're doing with the show.

00:08:44.122 --> 00:08:47.326
It is so wonderful and specific.
And I'm so happy it's back.

00:08:47.326 --> 00:08:48.827
And thank you guys
for being here.

00:08:48.827 --> 00:08:50.429
-Thank you. Thank you.

00:08:50.429 --> 00:08:52.831
-Fred Armisen, Ana Fabrega,
Julio Torres.

00:08:52.831 --> 00:08:56.335
"Los Espookys," Friday nights at
11:00 on HBO and HBO Max.

00:08:56.335 --> 00:08:58.404
We'll be right back with music
from Ashley McBryde.

